#!/usr/bin/env python3
# set-add -- canonical live per-set writer (B5/C2 structural forcing).
#
# The ONE write path for a single in-session set row. kit_life.py / kit_life.sh
# are FRAMEWORK code and must NOT be hand-patched, so the per-set write is a
# direct, idempotent sqlite3 UPSERT encapsulated here -- same discipline as
# session-closeout's tag write. Structured per-set data (weight/reps/rir) lands
# in the W02 workout_sets table; the paired set-table renderer reads it back so
# the in-session progress table is generated FROM STATE (no hand-typing, no
# dropped rows).
#
# Usage:
#   set-add <session_id> <exercise_name> <slot_num> <set_seq> <reps> \
#           [--weight <kg>] [--rir <int>] [--raw "<utterance>"] [--db PATH]
#
#   session_id     workouts.id (must already exist -- SESSION_START pre-creates it)
#   exercise_name  program.yaml slot name, snake_case (e.g. hammer_curl)
#   slot_num       program.yaml slot number (1-based), or `session` for a
#                  SESSION-level row (mat prep / cooldown: no program slot).
#                  Stored as NULL -- kit 061's own meaning of a missing slot.
#   set_seq        set order within the exercise. >= 1 = a WORKING set.
#                  <= 0 = a ramp / primer set (convention, see below), so a
#                  ramp row survives round-trip into the table without a schema
#                  change. set_seq 0 = bodyweight/empty-bar primer ("bw"),
#                  set_seq -N = ramp set N ("r1" = -1, "r2" = -2, ...).
#   reps           reps completed (integer, required)
#   --weight       working weight in kg. OMIT for a calibration/bodyweight set
#                  (stored NULL -> renders weight-free, no invented placeholder).
#
# WEIGHT RESOLUTION -- explicit precedence (2026-08-21, NULL-weight defect fix).
# On 2026-08-20 nine BARBELL working sets landed with weight_kg NULL and were
# silently dropped from every volume figure. Root cause: the BUTTON path carries
# the weight deterministically (the armed `cmd` argv in .idrill-arm/<arm_id>
# already contains `--weight 42.5`), but the TEXT path (owner types "6 2-3")
# carried none -- it relied on the MODEL composing --weight from conversation
# context, which produced nothing for all 9 sets. The weight is now a
# CODE-CARRIED value on BOTH routes:
#
#   1. an explicit --weight from the caller ALWAYS wins (button path, owner
#      correction, backfill tooling -- never second-guessed);
#   2. otherwise, for a WORKING set (set_seq >= 1) only, the live armed
#      prescription for this (session_id, exercise_name) is read out of
#      .idrill-arm/ (see _resolve_armed_weight -- exact-key arm first, then the
#      newest sibling arm of the SAME session+exercise, which carries the same
#      seq-independent prescription);
#   3. NULL survives ONLY for a genuinely weight-free movement -- see the
#      _plate_loaded_working_set guard below, which refuses the write outright
#      when a PLATE-LOADED working set would otherwise store NULL.
#
# Ramp/primer rows (set_seq <= 0) are NEVER auto-filled: their NULL is a real
# datum (empty-bar / bodyweight primer), so step 2 and the guard both skip them.
#   --rir          reps-in-reserve (integer). Omit if not reported (NULL).
#   --raw          original user utterance, stored for audit.
#   --db           path to kit_life.db (default: ../../database/kit_life.db).
#
# UPSERT: ON CONFLICT(session_id, exercise_name, set_seq) the row is REWRITTEN
# (F3a correction re-writes the same set slot, never a duplicate). recorded_at
# is refreshed on every write THAT ACTUALLY CHANGES THE SET -- a content-
# identical re-fire preserves it, see the CHRONOLOGY note in the collision gate
# below (2026-09-01 recorded_at-churn fix).
#
# SET-SEQ COLLISION GATE -- explicit precedence (2026-08-21, silent-overwrite
# defect fix). The UPSERT above used to overwrite a colliding row ALWAYS and
# SILENTLY. But set_seq is chosen by the MODEL, not computed by code, so any
# desync of the model's internal count (a weight override mid-exercise, a
# warm-up rung, an out-of-band correction) wrote the wrong set_seq and DESTROYED
# the row already sitting there. Three separate live losses on 2026-08-21: a
# warm-up landed as working set 1, then working set 2 landed on set_seq=1 and
# erased set 1 outright -- 2 sets performed, 1 row in the DB, recovered only by
# hand. Same class as the NULL-weight defect above: something that MUST hold was
# left to model discretion, so a deterministic carrier is required (doctrine D2).
# A write that collides with an existing row now resolves as:
#
#   1. IDENTICAL measurements (slot_num/weight_kg/reps/rir all equal) -- the
#      idempotent re-fire this UPSERT exists to support (the arm/callback path
#      can re-fire). ALLOWED, unchanged, exit 0. The row content cannot change,
#      so nothing can be lost. An additive `SET_SEQ_NOOP` line is printed AFTER
#      SET_LOGGED because "this write changed nothing" is exactly the signal a
#      caller needs when the owner actually just performed a NEW set (the row
#      survives, but the set COUNT would silently stay wrong otherwise);
#      CHRONOLOGY (2026-09-01 fix): "unchanged" now means the WHOLE row,
#      recorded_at included. The four MEASURED fields were the only things this
#      case compared, but the DO UPDATE below stamped `recorded_at =
#      datetime('now')` on every branch -- so the one write that provably
#      changed nothing was also the one that silently rewrote WHEN the set
#      happened. Live cost, session 10095 / incline_dumbbell_press: warm-up rung
#      r1 was logged FIRST and in the right order (rowid 10701, ahead of r2 and
#      of every working set), a later content-identical re-fire pushed its
#      recorded_at to 07:10:20 -- after three working sets -- and any reader
#      ordering by recorded_at then saw a ladder that had never been out of
#      order. One was, and reported a non-existent ordering defect to the owner.
#      A no-op re-fire therefore preserves recorded_at (and the original
#      raw_input utterance) verbatim; cases 2 and 3 are untouched, and a real
#      value change under case 3 still stamps now, because that write did
#      happen now;
#   2. DIFFERENT measurements and NO --correct -- REFUSED. Nothing is written,
#      the existing row is untouched, exit 1 + the `SET_SEQ_OCCUPIED` machine
#      marker and a `SEQ_ASK:` Korean line on STDOUT (see _refuse_occupied_seq),
#      same convention as the WEIGHT_MISSING / WEIGHT_ASK refusal. The refusal
#      carries the CODE-COMPUTED `next_free=` seq, so the caller never has to
#      count: it re-fires at next_free (it was a new set) or adds --correct (it
#      was a correction). Code owns the arithmetic, the caller owns the intent;
#      neither is guessed;
#   3. DIFFERENT measurements WITH --correct -- overwritten exactly as before
#      (the F3a in-place correction), plus a before->after `SET_OVERWRITE` audit
#      line on stderr so a rewrite is never invisible in the log.
#
# WHY REFUSE AND NOT AUTO-ADVANCE (case 2): auto-advancing to next_free would
# silently invent a set_seq. On a genuine in-place correction -- the documented
# post-arm re-entry path, where the owner re-taps the SAME set's keyboard with a
# fixed rep count -- auto-advance would manufacture a phantom set N+1 the owner
# never performed. That is the same defect class with the sign flipped (silent
# phantom data instead of silent lost data), and set-add cannot tell the two
# apart: intent lives with the caller, not in the row. So the arithmetic is
# code-carried and the intent decision is handed back, loudly, one turn.
#
# Warm-up / ramp rungs (set_seq <= 0) are guarded IDENTICALLY -- they collide the
# same way (incident 1 today was a warm-up), they hold real data, and they never
# arm a timer, so the gate costs them nothing. `next_free` is computed per RUNG
# LANE: working sets advance MAX(seq>=1)+1, ramp rungs advance MIN(seq<=-1)-1.
# The set_seq 0 (bw primer) lane holds exactly one row by convention, so it has
# no next_free -- the refusal reports `next_free=none` and the caller decides.
#
# STDOUT CONTRACT (the forcing hook keys on this exact well-formed line):
#   SET_LOGGED <session_id> <exercise_name> <set_seq> reps=<reps>
# matcher: ^SET_LOGGED\s+(\S+)\s+(\S+)\s+(-?\d+)\s+reps=\d+
# Only printed on a successful write (exit 0). session_id + exercise_name are on
# the line so the hook can order `set-table <session_id> <exercise_name>` next.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0 = set written (insert or upsert)
#   1 = error (unknown session id / bad args / MISSING WEIGHT refusal /
#       OCCUPIED SET_SEQ refusal / LADDER-MISMATCH SET_SEQ refusal) -- clear
#       stderr message. The missing-weight refusal additionally emits the machine
#       marker `WEIGHT_MISSING ...` + a `WEIGHT_ASK: <ko>` line on STDOUT (see
#       _refuse_missing_weight) so the owner's reps/RIR are never lost in
#       silence; the occupied-seq refusal emits `SET_SEQ_OCCUPIED ...` + a
#       `SEQ_ASK: <ko>` line the same way (see _refuse_occupied_seq), and the
#       ladder-mismatch refusal emits `SET_SEQ_MISMATCH ...` + `SEQ_ASK: <ko>`
#       (see _refuse_ladder_seq).
#
# stdlib only: sys, os, sqlite3, argparse, hashlib, json, time.

